  "cil_liability": false,
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed large HMO, accommodating up to 7 occupants, would significantly intensify activity compared to a single-family dwelling. The associated comings and goings of occupants, visitors, and vehicles would result in increased noise and disturbance, harming the established residential character of this densely built-up area. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policy CP5 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), Policies DMD5 and DMD8 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014) and the National Planning Policy Framework (2026).",
    "The proposed development, by reason of the excessive depth, height and proximity of the single-storey rear/side extension to the shared boundary, would result in an overbearing sense of enclosure, a materially worsened outlook and an unacceptable loss of daylight and sunlight to the No. 51 Gloucester Road. Furthermore, the intensification of the property as a 7-bedroom, 7-person large House in Multiple Occupation, together with the associated comings and goings of occupants, visitors and vehicles throughout the day and night, would generate unacceptable levels of noise and general disturbance. The combined impacts would cause significant harm to the living conditions and quiet enjoyment of neighbouring occupiers. The proposal is therefore contrary to Core Policy CP5 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010), Policies DMD5, DMD8 and DMD11 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014) and the National Planning Policy Framework (2026).",
    "The applicant has failed to demonstrate, through the submission of a robust parking stress survey and supporting evidence, that the additional parking demand arising from the proposed 7-bedroom, 7-person large House in Multiple Occupation (HMO) can be accommodated within the surrounding highway network without resulting in unacceptable parking stress, displacement parking, or harm to highway safety and the efficient operation of the local road network. In the absence of sufficient evidence to demonstrate that the transport impacts of the development would be acceptable, the proposal is contrary to the National Planning Policy Framework (2026), Policies T2, T3, T4 and T6 of the London Plan (2021), Core Policy CP24 of the Enfield Core Strategy (2010) and Policy DMD45 and DMD48 of the Enfield Development Management Document (2014).\r"
  ]
}